LEVEL 15 → 16 — SSL/TLS ENCRYPTED COMMUNICATION WITH OPENSSL


WHAT THIS LEVEL IS ABOUT : 

Similar to the previous level, but the service on localhost:30001 uses SSL/TLS encryption. Plain tools like nc will fail because they send unencrypted data. This level introduces openssl s_client — a tool for manually interacting with encrypted services.


APPROACH : 

I first tried Netcat, which produced garbled output because the server expected a TLS handshake. The correct tool was:
openssl s_client -connect localhost:30001
After the encrypted connection was established, I pasted the current level's password and pressed Enter. The server responded with the next password.

Command Breakdown
Part                   Meaning
openssl                Cryptographic operations toolkit
s_client               SSL/TLS client mode
-connect               Specify target host and port
localhost:30001        Target encrypted service

Why Other Methods Fail
Tool                    Why It Fails
nc localhost 30001      Sends plain text — server expects TLS handshake
telnet localhost 30001  Unencrypted protocol — incompatible with TLS

Password obtained. [Not shared per OverTheWire rules]


KEY TAKEAWAY : 

Not all services accept plain TCP communication. TLS is the encryption layer behind HTTPS, secure email, and most modern network services. openssl s_client is widely used in security testing to manually inspect and interact with encrypted services — for example, verifying a server's certificate or testing for SSL misconfigurations.
